# TypeScript & React Conventions

## General
- Use **strict** TypeScript; avoid `any` unless necessary and document why.
- Prefer `interface` for object shapes and `type` for unions/aliases.

## React
- Use **functional components**; extract reusable logic into custom hooks.
- Type props explicitly: `React.PropsWithChildren<{}>`, `React.ComponentProps<'div'>`, or named interfaces.
- For event handlers, use React types: `React.MouseEventHandler<HTMLButtonElement>`, etc.

## Imports
- Use path aliases: `@/src/...`, `@/assets/...`.
- Group: React/external libs first, then internal (components, lib, assets).

## Examples

```tsx
// ✅ Props with explicit interface
interface CardProps {
  title: string;
  children?: React.ReactNode;
}
function Card({ title, children }: CardProps) {
  return <div data-title={title}>{children}</div>;
}

// ✅ Next image result: use .src for CSS url()
function imgSrc(img: string | { src: string; height?: number; width?: number }): string {
  return typeof img === "string" ? img : img.src;
}
```

## Error handling
- In async code, log and rethrow or surface errors; avoid empty `catch` blocks.
